arm64ec: a notification hook must not block on the way in

Resident Evil Requiem deadlocks about twenty seconds in, with CPU and GPU
both flat at 0%. Wine's own critical-section timeout diagnostics name the
whole cycle:

    section 00000000004500D0 "ntdll/heap.c: main process heap section"
        wait timed out in thread 0280, blocked by 0188
    section 0000006FFE04D498 "?"
        wait timed out in thread 0188, blocked by 0280

The unnamed section is FEX's. libarm64ecfex.dll is mapped at 0x6FFDCB0000
and is 5,148,672 bytes, so 0x6FFE04D498 is 0x39D498 into its data, and
ThreadCreationMutex is a std::recursive_mutex -- which is what backs an
RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION and so what RtlpWaitForCriticalSection waits on.

Wine calls the memory and read notifications as a before/after pair around
the syscall, so locking in the "before" half holds the mutex across
NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtFreeVirtualMemory, NtProtectVirtualMemory,
NtUnmapViewOfSection and NtReadFile. That gives two opposite lock orders:

  0188  RtlAllocateHeap takes the process heap section, the heap needs to
        grow, NtAllocateVirtualMemory calls NotifyMemoryAlloc(After=FALSE)
        -- blocks on ThreadCreationMutex

  0280  LdrLoadDll holds the loader section, enters a notification hook and
        takes ThreadCreationMutex; the syscall Wine then performs allocates
        -- blocks on the process heap section

0184, 01f8 and 0284 pile up behind them. The trigger measured here is
DStorageCore.dll being mapped late in startup while other threads allocate,
which is why DirectStorage titles hit it so reliably -- but nothing about
the cycle is specific to that DLL.

try_lock breaks the edge Wine forces on us from inside a syscall, and
breaking either edge is enough. When the try fails the notification still
runs; the interval map is unaffected because InvalidationTracker takes
IntervalsLock around every mutation of it. What is given up is the coarse
serialisation against thread creation, which narrows to a race the two
halves of the pair already permitted.

The acquisition is tracked per thread and per nesting level rather than
with one flag: the pairs nest, and a failed try at one level must not
release a mutex that a different level acquired.

Not fixed here, and left deliberately: BTCpu64NotifyReadFile also holds
CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex() across the read, and NotifyMapViewOfSection
still blocks on a scoped_lock. Neither appeared in the measured cycle, and
neither can be made non-blocking without dropping work that has to happen.

VESSEL-LOCAL. native/fex/CLAUDE.md forbids AI-generated code in
contributions to FEX, so this must not be sent upstream.

diff --git a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
index ab3e7a35e..5e5de35d3 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
@@ -148,6 +148,69 @@ std::recursive_mutex ThreadCreationMutex;
 // Map of TIDs to their FEX thread state, `ThreadCreationMutex` must be locked when accessing
 std::unordered_map<DWORD, FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState*> Threads;
 
+// VESSEL: the memory notification hooks must never *block* on the way in.
+//
+// Wine calls NotifyMemory{Alloc,Free,Protect} and NotifyUnmapViewOfSection as a
+// before/after pair around the memory syscall itself, so taking the mutex in the
+// "before" half holds it across NtAllocateVirtualMemory and friends. That puts
+// FEX on both sides of a lock order it does not own, and the two orders are
+// opposite:
+//
+//   thread A  RtlAllocateHeap takes the process heap section, needs to grow the
+//             heap, calls NtAllocateVirtualMemory, and lands in
+//             NotifyMemoryAlloc(After=FALSE) -- blocks on ThreadCreationMutex
+//   thread B  inside LdrLoadDll (loader section held) enters a notify hook and
+//             takes ThreadCreationMutex; the syscall Wine then performs itself
+//             allocates -- blocks on the process heap section
+//
+// Measured on device with Resident Evil Requiem loading DStorageCore.dll:
+//
+//   section ...4500D0 "heap.c: main process heap section" blocked by 0188
+//   section ...E04D498 "?"  (this mutex)                  blocked by 0280
+//
+// 0280 held the loader section and this mutex while waiting on the heap, 0188
+// held the heap while waiting on this mutex, and 0184/01f8/0284 piled up behind
+// them. CPU and GPU both flatlined at 0%.
+//
+// try_lock cannot close that cycle: breaking either edge is enough, and this
+// breaks the one Wine forces on us from inside a syscall. When the try fails the
+// notification runs without the coarse mutex, which is safe for the interval map
+// itself -- InvalidationTracker takes IntervalsLock around every mutation. What
+// is given up is coarse serialisation against thread creation, narrowing to a
+// race that the two halves of the pair already allowed.
+//
+// The acquisition is tracked per thread and per nesting level rather than with a
+// single flag, because the pairs nest and a failed try at one level must not
+// unlock a mutex a different level acquired.
+constexpr uint32_t NotifyLockMaxDepth = 64;
+thread_local uint64_t NotifyLockAcquired = 0;
+thread_local uint32_t NotifyLockDepth = 0;
+
+// Enter the "before" half of a notification pair without ever blocking.
+void NotifyLockEnter() {
+  const uint32_t Level = NotifyLockDepth++;
+  if (Level >= NotifyLockMaxDepth) {
+    return;
+  }
+  if (ThreadCreationMutex.try_lock()) {
+    NotifyLockAcquired |= (1ull << Level);
+  } else {
+    NotifyLockAcquired &= ~(1ull << Level);
+  }
+}
+
+// Leave the "after" half, releasing only if this level actually acquired.
+void NotifyLockLeave() {
+  if (!NotifyLockDepth) {
+    return;
+  }
+  const uint32_t Level = --NotifyLockDepth;
+  if (Level < NotifyLockMaxDepth && (NotifyLockAcquired & (1ull << Level))) {
+    NotifyLockAcquired &= ~(1ull << Level);
+    NotifyLockLeave();
+  }
+}
+
 std::pair<NTSTATUS, ThreadCPUArea> GetThreadCPUArea(HANDLE Thread) {
   THREAD_BASIC_INFORMATION Info;
   const NTSTATUS Err = NtQueryInformationThread(Thread, ThreadBasicInformation, &Info, sizeof(Info), nullptr);
@@ -784,13 +847,13 @@ void NotifyMemoryAlloc(void* Address, SIZE_T Size, ULONG Type, ULONG Prot, BOOL
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter();
   } else {
     // MEM_RESET(_UNDO) ignores the passed permissions
     if (!Status && !(Type & (MEM_RESET | MEM_RESET_UNDO))) {
       InvalidationTracker->HandleMemoryProtectionNotification(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size), Prot);
     }
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave();
   }
 }
 
@@ -800,12 +863,12 @@ void NotifyMemoryFree(void* Address, SIZE_T Size, ULONG FreeType, BOOL After, NT
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter();
   } else {
     if (!Status) {
       InvalidationTracker->InvalidateAlignedInterval(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size), true);
     }
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave();
   }
 }
 
@@ -815,12 +878,12 @@ void NotifyMemoryProtect(void* Address, SIZE_T Size, ULONG NewProt, BOOL After,
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter();
   } else {
     if (!Status) {
       InvalidationTracker->HandleMemoryProtectionNotification(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size), NewProt);
     }
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave();
   }
 }
 
@@ -844,13 +907,13 @@ void NotifyUnmapViewOfSection(void* Address, BOOL After, NTSTATUS Status) {
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter();
     auto [Start, Size] = InvalidationTracker->InvalidateContainingSection(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), true);
     if (Size) {
       HandleImageUnmap(Start, Size);
     }
   } else {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave();
   }
 }
 
@@ -889,19 +952,19 @@ void BTCpu64NotifyReadFile(HANDLE Handle, void* Address, SIZE_T Size, BOOL After
 
   auto& InLockedRWXRead = GetFrontendThreadData(ThreadState)->InLockedRWXRead;
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter();
     CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().lock();
     if (InvalidationTracker->BeginUntrackedWriteLocked(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size))) {
       InLockedRWXRead = true;
     } else {
       CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().unlock();
-      ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+      NotifyLockLeave();
     }
   } else {
     if (InLockedRWXRead) {
       InLockedRWXRead = false;
       CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().unlock();
-      ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+      NotifyLockLeave();
     }
   }
 }
